:NISTAIRMFExplainability rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                  :ExplainabilityObligation ;
                         :hasEvidence :NISTAIRMFExplainabilityEvidence ;
                         rdfs:comment "NIST AI RMF is a voluntary framework, so this is a recommended trustworthiness characteristic rather than a binding legal obligation. Recorded here because competency question 3 asks which frameworks require explainability, and the comparison is only meaningful if the voluntary/binding distinction is visible."@en ;
                         rdfs:label "NIST AI RMF explainable and interpretable characteristic"@en .

:NISTAISystemDefinition rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                 :DefinitionStatement ;
                        :definesConcept :AISystem ;
                        :definitionText "an engineered or machine-based system that can, for a given set of objectives, generate outputs such as predictions, recommendations, or decisions influencing real or virtual environments. AI systems are designed to operate with varying levels of autonomy" ;
                        :hasEvidence :NISTAIRMFDefinitionEvidence ;
                        :statedBy :NIST_AI_RMF ;
                        rdfs:comment "NIST attributes this wording to the OECD Recommendation on AI (2019) and ISO/IEC 22989:2022, so the NIST and EU definitions share an ancestor. Both centre on a machine-based system generating outputs at varying levels of autonomy; the EU adds post-deployment adaptiveness and explicit or implicit objectives."@en ;
                        rdfs:label "NIST AI RMF definition of 'AI system'"@en .

:WestpacAISystemDefinition rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                    :DefinitionStatement ;
                           :definesConcept :AISystem ;
                           :definitionText "A machine-based system designed to operate with varying levels of autonomy and that may exhibit adaptiveness after deployment and that, for explicit or implicit objectives, infers, from the input it receives, how to generate outputs such as predictions, content, recommendations, or decisions that can influence physical or virtual environments." ;
                           :hasEvidence :WestpacAIDefinitionEvidence ;
                           :statedBy :Westpac ;
                           rdfs:comment "The first firm-stated definition of AI collected in this study. The wording is that of EU AI Act Article 3(1) with only punctuation and connective differences, yet the page cites no source for it. This is the clearest single instance of the unattributed adoption pattern the comparative analysis reports."@en ;
                           rdfs:label "Westpac definition of 'AI'"@en .

